WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.377
Start with the branching breast ducts and lobules.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.110
Now move closer to the duct wall.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.850
The basement membrane is the boundary that matters in

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:09.604
this comparison.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.860
Ductal carcinoma in situ is limited to the breast

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.908
ducts and lobules without
invading the basement membrane.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.255
Watch abnormal cells accumulate inside the boundary.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.033
Their number can increase
while that boundary remains intact.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.537
Invasive carcinoma crosses into surrounding tissue.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.420
Watch cells extend beyond the duct boundary in the

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.300
second comparison.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.577
These are different disease states.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.130
The supplied slides do not establish that every in

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.945
situ lesion will become invasive.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.660
The released breast case
then distinguishes a breast cancer

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.465
deposit in the lung from a new primary lung

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:44.890
cancer.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.680
A tumor found in the lung can still have

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:48.457
a breast origin.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.460
Keep location, invasion, and
tumor origin separate when interpreting

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:53.945
the case.
